            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>A Typical Scenario: The Client Asked Three Times, but the Team Could Not Find the Latest Status</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team applied for advertising accounts on Meta, Google Ads, and TikTok. After receiving the client’s documents, the account-opening consultant sent identification files, company information, and business registration documents to different chat groups, then tracked the review process in a personal spreadsheet.</p>
<p>The next day, the client asked when the account would be ready for payment funding. The consultant could not locate the relevant request and had to contact customer support again. Two days later, another team member resubmitted the client’s documents. Even after the account was approved, no one promptly shared the advertising account ID, funding permissions, or operating instructions. The client interpreted the repeated delays as poor responsiveness, while the agency manager could not identify which cases required immediate attention.</p>
<p>This was not merely a record-keeping problem. The agency lacked a unified operational view of its account-opening service.</p>
<h2>First, Define What Must Appear on the Dashboard</h2>
<p>Account opening is not a one-time submission. It is a service chain involving document collection, platform review, account configuration, funding, and ongoing risk control. Five groups of information should be standardized:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Client and requirements:</strong> Record the legal entity, target platform, intended advertising regions, budget range, and planned opening date to avoid mixing similarly named companies or different requests.</li>
<li><strong>Documents and tasks:</strong> Show whether each document is complete, who owns the follow-up, what information is missing, and when the status was last updated.</li>
<li><strong>Review progress:</strong> Distinguish submitted, under review, additional information required, approved, and rejected instead of using a vague “in progress” label.</li>
<li><strong>Accounts and permissions:</strong> Centralize the advertising account ID, agency access, funding permissions, payment method, and authorized users.</li>
<li><strong>Funding and exceptions:</strong> Track funding orders, payment status, balance changes, and risk signals such as inconsistent information, repeated submissions, or unusual access attempts.</li>
</ul>
<p>The purpose of a dashboard is not simply to replace a spreadsheet. It is to create a shared operating standard. Statuses should have designated owners, key milestones should trigger reminders, and sensitive information should be access-controlled. Clients should only see information related to their own account.</p>
<h2>Then, Convert the Workflow into an Executable SOP</h2>
<h3>1. Pre-check documents when creating the case</h3>
<p>Before submitting a request, check whether the legal entity name, registered address, payment information, and campaign objectives are consistent. If anything is missing, provide one consolidated checklist and set a deadline for additional materials. This reduces delays caused by repeated document requests during platform review.</p>
<h3>2. Use task cards instead of chat-based follow-ups</h3>
<p>Create a separate task card for every application, with an owner, current status, pending actions, and the next committed update time. If a case passes its deadline without an update, the dashboard should notify a manager. The timeline also allows another team member to take over without rebuilding the case history.</p>
<h3>3. Connect accounts, funding, and risk controls</h3>
<p>After approval, do not simply send an “account opened” notification. The consultant should also confirm whether the client can fund and use the account, and explain how the balance, billing, payment entity, and advertising account are connected. Requests involving large funding amounts, frequent payment-method changes, or repeated changes to the funding entity should be routed to manual review.</p>
<h3>4. Share progress externally while preserving an internal audit trail</h3>
<p>Clients usually do not need internal notes, but they should be able to see their document checklist, review stage, and expected next action. Internally, the system should preserve submission records, status changes, operator details, and risk labels for financial reconciliation and future appeals.</p>
<h2>The Result: Deliver by Milestones Instead of Chasing Updates</h2>
<p>In the anonymous scenario above, the team adopted a unified dashboard. Client documents, review milestones, and account permissions became visible in one place. Task statuses prevented duplicate submissions, overdue cases appeared in an alert queue, and funding personnel could verify the relationship between the payment entity and the advertising account before processing a transaction.</p>
<p>The outcome should not be described with exaggerated claims such as “double the efficiency.” More reliable indicators are whether the team reduced repetitive communication, identified stalled cases earlier, gave clients a clear next step, and enabled finance staff to reconcile funding records quickly.</p>
<p>The competitiveness of an agency account-opening service ultimately depends on more than whether it can submit documents. It depends on whether the entire process is consistent, traceable, and collaborative. A systemized dashboard connects client delivery, account administration, and advertising risk control, ensuring that every opening request has a record and every status change has a clear basis.</p>
